WEBVTT


00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.470
We often think of pin tucks as
horizontal lines across our knitting.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.590
But we could do so much
more with this technique.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:13.140
To review: pin tucks on the ribber
are formed with full needle rib.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.020
All needles in work on both beds.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.980
One of the beds is set to
slip for a few rows while the

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.750
other bed continues to knit.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.380
The additional rows that are knit
will bubble up and form texture.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:34.475
If we slip on the main bed and use punch
cards or electronic stitch patterns, we

00:00:34.475 --> 00:00:36.665
can create lots of different textures.

00:00:37.595 --> 00:00:41.285
In other words, we don't
have to slip an entire row.

00:00:42.095 --> 00:00:47.075
Selectively slipping groups of needles
on the main bed while continuing

00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:51.334
to knit the rows on the ribber,
we'll create texture like this.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.900
Take a close look at this stitch pattern.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.280
It's mostly black.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.510
The white holes are needles
that will be slipping.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.129
Remember, slip stitch means
the needle will not knit.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.550
And the working yarn will
pass UNDER the needle.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:14.130
When knitting on a single bed, this
forms floats across the purl side of the

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.680
fabric, and we often use the stranded
side as the public side of our knitting.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:26.310
Because we're working full needle rib, the
ribber's stitches will cover the floats.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.340
Texture will form when groups of needles
are slipped on the main bed and the

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.010
ribber continues to knit all  rows.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.310
In this stitch pattern,
I slipped for four rows.

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:42.360
It created diagonal raised
areas of texture on the

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.039
ribber side of the knitting.

00:01:45.615 --> 00:01:50.115
Looking closely, you can see
the distorted slip stitches on

00:01:50.115 --> 00:01:51.824
the reverse side of the fabric.

00:01:51.914 --> 00:01:54.375
This is the main bed side of the knitting.

00:01:55.664 --> 00:01:57.675
Are you starting to
see some possibilities?

00:01:58.964 --> 00:02:01.335
Here's a more complicated stitch pattern.

00:02:01.875 --> 00:02:05.175
The needles were selected according
to the pattern and slipped.

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:06.794
As they were slipped,

00:02:06.794 --> 00:02:11.715
the ribber needles continued to knit,
creating this interesting graphic texture.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.220
Depending on your machine and
your yarn here are some tips.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.899
It's recommended that you use the
fine knit bar on the main bed.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.190
This raises the needles slightly
and can help with the knitting.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.720
Play with the stitch
tensions between the beds.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.000
If the main bed tension is too
loose, those strands can get sloppy

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.620
and catch on the sinker posts.

00:02:39.614 --> 00:02:43.684
I had difficulty with this
yarn on my brother, 970.

00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:49.065
Looking closely, there are sections
that are slipping 8 to 10 rows,

00:02:49.725 --> 00:02:54.255
and I determined this was just too
many for my machine with this yarn.

00:02:55.305 --> 00:02:59.705
In this example, the maximum
rows that are slipped are 4.

00:03:00.915 --> 00:03:01.155
This

00:03:01.155 --> 00:03:05.535
doesn't give as much texture,
but it knit a lot more cleanly.

00:03:06.465 --> 00:03:10.305
When choosing or creating a pin
tuck pattern, please keep the

00:03:10.305 --> 00:03:16.765
number of slipped rows in mind
for your machine and your yarn.

